### greenlet implements coroutines by moving the C stack out from under the
### interpreter: it saves the live stack of the outgoing greenlet to the heap,
### adjusts the stack pointer, and copies the incoming greenlet's stack back to
### the same addresses. Everything platform-specific lives in one header that
### defines slp_switch(), and this port adds the z/OS one.
###
### Why the Linux/s390x file could not be reused. z/OS defines __s390x__, so it
### looks like a match, but it is not: on this platform the linkage is XPLINK,
### and XPLINK disagrees with the Linux ELF ABI about every fact this code
### depends on.
###
###   - The stack pointer is r4. r15 -- what the Linux file reads -- holds
###     something unrelated. Verified directly: with a local at 0x50082FF9F0,
###     r4 was 0x50082FF060 and r15 was 0x1A70C010.
###   - Callee-saved is r6-r15, emitted as one "stmg 6,15,<off>(4)".
###   - The stack grows down, but r4 points 2048 bytes BELOW the frame it
###     addresses, so frame storage is at positive displacements from r4.
###   - The return address is r7 and a call returns via "b 2(7)".
###
### The assembler behind inline asm is HLASM, not gas. An instruction must not
### start in column 1 or it is read as a label -- which fails as "expected
### identifier" on the first one and "symbol is already defined" on the second
### -- and register operands are bare numbers, so "%0" expands to "4", not
### "%r4". Every instruction in the header starts with a space.
###
### The part that took longest to find, and the reason this port exists at all:
### on XPLINK a callee saves its incoming registers into its CALLER's frame,
### 1808 bytes ABOVE the caller's stack pointer. greenlet assumes the opposite
### -- that everything above the stack pointer is the greenlet's saved state
### and everything below is scratch. So slp_restore_state(), the very function
### called to copy the incoming stack back, parks its own return address inside
### the bytes it is about to overwrite. The symptom is not a crash at the
### memcpy; it is the callee returning 0 and the caller reading 7104.
###
### The fix is to drop the stack pointer clear of the restore region across
### that one call, which the header does with a 4096-byte guard. The generated
### code was checked: no r4-relative access appears between the two adjustments,
### so the guard is sound rather than lucky.
###
### The second blocker is unrelated to stacks: this compiler supports no
### thread-local storage at all. thread_local, _Thread_local and __thread are
### all rejected with "thread-local storage is not supported for the current
### target". greenlet has exactly one thread-local, so the patch expresses it
### with a pthread key whose destructor runs at thread exit -- the same
### create-on-first-use, destroy-at-thread-exit semantics.
###
### THIS PORT IS NOT COMPLETE. Core switching works and a large part of the
### test suite passes, but several test modules still kill the process. Read
### the README before depending on it; zopen_install_caveats() below carries
### the short version.

GREENLET_VERSION="3.5.5"

# bump: greenlet-version /GREENLET_VERSION="(.*)"/ https://github.com/python-greenlet/greenlet.git|semver:*

export ZOPEN_STABLE_URL="https://github.com/python-greenlet/greenlet.git"
export ZOPEN_STABLE_TAG="${GREENLET_VERSION}"
export ZOPEN_STABLE_DEPS="check_python curl git patch"

export ZOPEN_DEV_URL="https://github.com/python-greenlet/greenlet.git"
export ZOPEN_DEV_DEPS="${ZOPEN_STABLE_DEPS}"

# greenlet has no runtime dependencies of its own.
export ZOPEN_RUNTIME_DEPS="check_python"

export ZOPEN_CATEGORIES="development library"

export ZOPEN_PYTHON_VERSIONS="${ZOPEN_PYTHON_VERSIONS:-3.12 3.13 3.14}"
export ZOPEN_BUILD_SYSTEM="Python"

# The extension is C++ and is built per interpreter, so unlike a pure-Python
# port this produces one wheel per ABI (cp312, cp313, cp314).
#
# ZOPEN_COMP is still "skip": setup.py drives the compiler itself through
# sysconfig, and the flags it picks up are the ones this extension needs.
# Naming a compiler here only makes zopen-build generate and compile its zoslib
# environment hooks, which this port has no use for and which fails with the
# compiler unset:
#
#   -DPATH_MAX=1024: eval: ... FSUM7351 not found
#   ***ERROR: Compiler command for zoslib_env_hook.c failed to compile
export ZOPEN_COMP="skip"
export ZOPEN_CONFIGURE="skip"

zopen_get_version() {
  echo "${GREENLET_VERSION}"
}

# greenlet's own suite is not run here, and neither is a single in-process
# check script. On this platform a failing greenlet does not raise -- it takes
# the process down, with a 0C4 or a bare SIGKILL -- so one bad behaviour would
# hide every result after it. The first version of this check did exactly that:
# it died at PyType_IsSubtype on an isinstance() call and reported nothing at
# all for any interpreter.
#
# Each behaviour therefore runs in its own subprocess. A scenario that kills its
# process is a FAIL with a return code, and the rest still report.
zopen_python_test() {
  GREENLET_EXPECTED_VERSION="${GREENLET_VERSION}" python - <<'PYEOF'
import os
import subprocess
import sys

print("Interpreter: Python %s" % sys.version.split()[0])

PRELUDE = (
    "from greenlet import greenlet as G\n"
    "import greenlet\n"
    "def switch(*a):\n"
    "    return G.getcurrent().parent.switch(*a)\n"
)

SCENARIOS = [
    ("version is the one this port builds",
     "assert greenlet.__version__ == %r, greenlet.__version__"
     % os.environ["GREENLET_EXPECTED_VERSION"]),

    ("the C extension is the one imported",
     "from greenlet import _greenlet\n"
     "assert _greenlet.__file__.endswith(('.so', '.x')), _greenlet.__file__"),

    ("native byte order is big-endian",
     "import sys; assert sys.byteorder == 'big', sys.byteorder"),

    ("a greenlet runs and returns a value",
     "g = G(lambda: 42)\n"
     "assert g.switch() == 42\n"
     "assert g.dead"),

    ("control alternates between parent and child",
     "log = []\n"
     "def child():\n"
     "    log.append('c1'); switch(); log.append('c2'); return 'done'\n"
     "g = G(child)\n"
     "log.append('m1'); g.switch(); log.append('m2')\n"
     "r = g.switch(); log.append('m3')\n"
     "assert log == ['m1','c1','m2','c2','m3'], log\n"
     "assert r == 'done'"),

    ("values survive a switch in both directions",
     "def echo():\n"
     "    return switch('from-child')\n"
     "g = G(echo)\n"
     "assert g.switch() == 'from-child'\n"
     "assert g.switch('from-parent') == 'from-parent'"),

    ("a large stack frame survives save and restore",
     "payload = list(range(4096))\n"
     "def worker():\n"
     "    local = list(payload)\n"
     "    switch()\n"
     "    assert local == payload\n"
     "    return sum(local)\n"
     "g = G(worker)\n"
     "g.switch()\n"
     "assert g.switch() == sum(payload)"),

    ("an exception propagates out of a greenlet",
     "def boom():\n"
     "    raise ValueError('from inside')\n"
     "g = G(boom)\n"
     "try:\n"
     "    g.switch()\n"
     "except ValueError as e:\n"
     "    assert str(e) == 'from inside'\n"
     "else:\n"
     "    raise AssertionError('did not propagate')"),

    ("throw() raises inside a suspended greenlet",
     "def child():\n"
     "    try:\n"
     "        switch()\n"
     "    except RuntimeError:\n"
     "        return 'caught'\n"
     "    return 'not-thrown'\n"
     "g = G(child)\n"
     "g.switch()\n"
     "assert g.throw(RuntimeError) == 'caught'"),

    ("greenlets nest",
     "order = []\n"
     "def inner():\n"
     "    order.append('inner'); return 'inner-done'\n"
     "def outer():\n"
     "    order.append('outer')\n"
     "    order.append(G(inner).switch())\n"
     "    return 'outer-done'\n"
     "g = G(outer)\n"
     "assert g.switch() == 'outer-done'\n"
     "assert order == ['outer','inner','inner-done'], order"),

    ("500 consecutive switches",
     "def child():\n"
     "    while True:\n"
     "        switch()\n"
     "g = G(child)\n"
     "g.switch()\n"
     "for _ in range(500):\n"
     "    g.switch()"),

    ("a producer/consumer pattern works",
     "def producer():\n"
     "    for i in range(50):\n"
     "        switch(i)\n"
     "g = G(producer)\n"
     "seen = [g.switch()]\n"
     "while not g.dead:\n"
     "    seen.append(g.switch())\n"
     "assert seen[:50] == list(range(50)), seen[:5]"),

]

# The outstanding defect is NOT probed with a hand-written micro-scenario.
# One was tried and it is not a reliable detector: whether any particular
# sequence dies depends on where its frames happen to land, so the same few
# lines pass in one build and kill the process in the next. Reporting such a
# scenario as PASS would read as "the defect is fixed" when nothing had changed.
#
# greenlet's own suite is the honest probe, so the modules known to die are run
# here -- each in its own process, since that is the only way to survive them --
# and reported without being counted. The set that fails shifts between builds
# for the same reason, which is itself the thing to watch.
KNOWN_BROKEN_MODULES = [
    "test_greenlet",
    "test_throw",
    "test_tracing",
    "test_greenlet_trash",
    "test_extension_interface",
]

passed = 0
failed = 0
known_broken_failed = 0

for name, body in SCENARIOS:
    code = PRELUDE + body + "\nprint('SCENARIO-OK')\n"
    proc = subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-c", code],
                          stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT)
    ok = proc.returncode == 0 and b"SCENARIO-OK" in proc.stdout
    if ok:
        print("PASS: %s" % name)
        passed += 1
    else:
        detail = proc.stdout.decode("latin-1", "replace").strip().splitlines()
        detail = detail[-1] if detail else "(no output)"
        print("FAIL: %s -- rc=%s %s" % (name, proc.returncode, detail))
        failed += 1

# Report the state of the known-broken modules without counting them.
#
# unittest rather than pytest: pytest has to collect through --pyargs, which in
# this venv resolves to a tests package it cannot import, and every module then
# came back as "rc=4, no tests ran". Reporting that as KILLED would be a lie of
# exactly the kind this port is trying not to tell -- it means "could not run",
# not "died". The three states are kept apart below.
# greenlet's tests package imports psutil at module scope, so without it every
# module comes back as an ImportError that unittest reports as rc=1 -- which
# looks exactly like "ran and had failures". Install it first; it is a zopen
# port, so it comes from the wheel index.
subprocess.run(
    [sys.executable, "-m", "pip", "install", "--quiet",
     "--disable-pip-version-check", "--only-binary=:all:",
     "--extra-index-url", "https://repo.zopen.community/pypi/wheels/simple/",
     "psutil", "objgraph"],
    stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT)

ran_none = 0
for mod in KNOWN_BROKEN_MODULES:
    proc = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-m", "unittest", "greenlet.tests." + mod, "-v"],
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT)
    out = proc.stdout.decode("latin-1", "replace")
    tail = out.strip().splitlines()
    tail = tail[-1] if tail else "(no output)"
    rc = proc.returncode

    # Order matters. An import error exits 1, the same code as "ran and some
    # tests failed", so it has to be recognised before rc is trusted -- calling
    # that "survived" would report a probe that never ran as a clean result.
    if "ImportError" in out or "ModuleNotFoundError" in out or "Ran 0 tests" in out:
        state = "could-not-run"
        ran_none += 1
    elif rc in (0, 1):
        # 0 = all passed, 1 = test failures. Either way the process lived,
        # which is what is being measured here.
        state = "survived"
    else:
        state = "KILLED"
        known_broken_failed += 1
    print("KNOWN-ISSUE: %-26s %-14s rc=%-5s %s"
          % (mod, state, rc, tail[:60]))

if known_broken_failed == 0 and ran_none == 0:
    print("KNOWN-ISSUE: every module that used to be killed survived this run. "
          "That may mean the defect is fixed, or only that the frames landed "
          "differently. Re-run the full suite before believing it.")
elif ran_none:
    print("KNOWN-ISSUE: %d module(s) could not be run at all, so this build "
          "says nothing about them" % ran_none)

import greenlet
print("greenlet %s on Python %s -- %d passed, %d failed, %d module(s) killed"
      % (greenlet.__version__, sys.version.split()[0],
         passed, failed, known_broken_failed))
sys.exit(1 if failed else 0)
PYEOF
}

zopen_check_results() {
  dir="$1"; pfx="$2"; chk="${dir}/${pfx}_check.log"
  passed=$(grep -c "^PASS:" "${chk}" 2> /dev/null)
  failed=$(grep -c "^FAIL:" "${chk}" 2> /dev/null)
  passed=${passed:-0}
  failed=${failed:-0}

  # An interpreter whose script dies before its first check contributes no PASS
  # and no FAIL, and on this port dying is a live possibility, so a crash must
  # not be scored as a clean run.
  interpreters=$(grep -c "Running tests with Python" "${chk}" 2> /dev/null)
  reported=$(grep -c "^greenlet .* on Python" "${chk}" 2> /dev/null)
  interpreters=${interpreters:-0}
  reported=${reported:-0}
  silent=$((interpreters - reported))
  if [ "${silent}" -gt 0 ]; then
    failed=$((failed + silent))
  fi

cat <<ZZ
actualFailures:${failed}
totalTests:$((passed + failed))
expectedFailures:0
ZZ
}

# zopen-build captures stdout from here into metadata.json as installation
# caveats. Anyone who installs this and never opens the README sees only this,
# so it leads with the thing that can take their process down.
zopen_install_caveats() {
  cat <<'CAVEATEOF'
This greenlet is incomplete. Do not put it in production yet.

Core switching works: greenlets run, values cross switches in both directions,
exceptions propagate out, throw() raises inside a suspended greenlet, greenlets
nest, and 500 consecutive switches are clean.

What does not work: some sequences kill the process outright -- SIGKILL, no
traceback, no dump. The smallest known reproducer is a switch, then a
reasonably deep Python call, then a throw:

    g.switch()
    unittest.TestCase().assertEqual(r, "ok")   # any deep-ish call
    g.throw(RuntimeError)                      # process dies here

Replace the middle line with a bare assert and it passes, so it is the depth of
the intervening call rather than the throw itself. Five of greenlet's own test
modules die this way, including its main suite.

The practical consequence: anything built on greenlet -- gevent, SQLAlchemy's
async support -- should be assumed affected. Use this port to develop and to
help finish the work, not to run anything that matters.

See the README for what was diagnosed, what was fixed, and where the remaining
defect has been narrowed to.
CAVEATEOF
}
